>> title: Vindaloo
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: pork, goan, vinegar, chiles, curry
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: vindalho, pork vindaloo, chicken vindaloo, vindaloo curry, vindaley
>> pairs-with: vindaloo-paste, pilau-rice, raita
>> servings: 6
>> time: 14 hr
>> step.1: marinate overnight, 12 hr
>> step.2: sear 8 min in batches
>> step.3: fry in 5 min
>> step.4: simmer 1 hr, covered, until a fork turns in it

Marinate @pork shoulder{2 1/2%lb}(1.1 kg; cut in 1 1/2-in cubes, some fat left on) with @vindaloo paste{1%cup}(240 g; the paste recipe) and @kosher salt{2%tsp} ~marinate{12%hr} in the fridge, turning it once if you are up.

Sear @&(~1)marinated pork{} in @ghee{2%Tbs}(28 g) in a #heavy pot{} ~sear{8%min}, in two batches, scraping the paste off the bottom before it catches.

Fry @onion-tomato masala{1%cup}(240 g; the base recipe) and @bay leaves{2} into @&(~1)seared pork{} ~fry{5%min}.

Simmer @&(~1)pot{} with @water{1%cup}(240 mL), @jaggery{1%Tbs}(15 g; or dark brown sugar) and @palm vinegar{2%Tbs}(30 mL) covered ~simmer{60%min}, until a fork turns in the meat without argument and the fat has come up red.

Pork is the original and still the best of it: the fat takes the vinegar in a way chicken cannot, and Goa is a place where pork is eaten. Chicken vindaloo is the English board's line and wants half the time. There is no potato in this — aloo in the name is a coincidence, the word is alhos, garlic — though half the shops in Britain put one in and nobody has ever sent it back. It is better on the second day, which is what the vinegar was for in the first place.
